  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday Night through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1130 AM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nGoing into Saturday, mid and high clouds will thicken across the\narea, but the daytime hours will still be quite warm.\n\nThe Saturday evening candy grab looks dry as models continue to \ndelay rain chances until later Saturday night. A few sprinkles \ncould creep into areas southwest of Nashville just after dark, but\nit looks like nothing to worry about. The evening will be cloudy \nand mild with temperatures in the lower 70s around 6 PM, falling \nto the lower 60s by midnight.\n\nAs the ridge moves out and a trough / axis of deep moisture\narrives, rain chances will jump late Saturday night into Sunday \nmorning. We are confident the system will bring rain, but it does \nnot look impressive in terms of rainfall amounts or instability. \nThe latest rainfall forecast shows less than one inch, so not \nreally much drought relief. A rumble of thunder could occur, but\nwe are not expecting any significant thunderstorm activity.\n\nMost of the rain will taper off by Sunday evening with dry and\nmild conditions returning for Monday. \n\nA stronger weather system moves in late Tuesday or Wednesday with\na better chance for soaking rainfall. Right now models are \nshowing 1 to 2 inch rainfall amounts. I would not bet on those \ntotals, but the system does look large and strong enough to at \nleast slow or pause the worsening drought. Once again, storm \npotential looks low with meager cape values indicated.\n\nBeyond midweek, the pattern will stay mild. The atmosphere will be\na little more wavy, so we have some hope for rain-making systems \nto continue coming into the area.",
